  [1mThe fork:[0m how a task becomes one you get notified about.

  - [1mExplicit watch[0m — a star/bell on each task; you subscribe by clicking. Buys: the clearest demo, since the click [3mis[0m
    the story you're showing. Costs: a per-task affordance, and a dead empty state until something is watched. Rules out
    nothing.
  - [1mImplicit by relationship[0m — you're the assignee, the creator, or you commented. Buys: what real tools (GitHub, Jira,
    Linear) actually do, so it feels familiar. Costs: the fake tasks now need assignees, authors, and comments to exist
    — noticeably more model surface for a demo.
  - [1mBoth[0m — implicit default, explicit watch/unwatch as override. Buys: the most realistic behavior. Costs: both of the
    above, plus the "muted but assigned" edge case, which is where real systems get confusing.
  - [1mEverything, muted later[0m — notify on all changes, let users mute. Buys: the simplest model. Costs: "tasks they care
    about" isn't really modeled at all — it's a firehose with an off switch.

  All four store subscriptions the same way (a set of task ids per user in local storage), so this is [1mcheap to change [0m
  [1mlater[0m — there's no migration cost worth worrying about in a prototype.

  [1mMy recommendation: explicit watch.[0m For a demo, the subscribe gesture is the most legible part of the concept, and it's
  the least fake data to invent. Implicit-by-assignee is the more realistic model, but it only looks realistic if I
  also invent assignees and authors, and that's model surface serving the fiction rather than the notifications.
